From 72e1199d3f99c8d8ef439f66811f507d6b736627 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 11:27:48 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 021/118] urltest: leave a member when its connections fail,
 not at the next test

Upstream urltest learns that a member is broken only from its periodic
URL test; a dead server fails every new connection for up to an interval
(3 minutes by default) plus the test timeout.

A failed dial through the selected member, or a failed write that
carried the handshake the dial deferred, is now evidence about it:

- A TCP dial of a protocol that contacts only its server before the first
  payload (VLESS, VMess, Trojan, Shadowsocks, ShadowTLS, Hysteria,
  Hysteria2, TUIC, AnyTLS, Naive) counts as the failed URL test it
  equals: the member becomes unavailable, the group selects again.
- Members that reach the destination when they dial (socks, http,
  direct, groups) and failed packet listens get one URL test, one at a
  time per member, paused after a false alarm by the test's length,
  doubling, at most an interval, until an interval without failures.
- A failed dial is not judged when another dial through the member
  succeeded after it began. Failures the caller caused say nothing.

The selection is stored atomically, and a switch is logged at warn
after the reason.
